---
title: Flags & experiments
summary: Kill switches, rollouts, targeting, and A/B tests as ONE primitive — flag(). Decided per visitor, server-side during SSR, zero JS shipped for the decision. OpenFeature plugs in through one decide() call; csr=false pages A/B for free.
related:
  - app/server-router
  - app/federation
  - regions/timing-nesting
---

A kill switch, a staged rollout, a targeting rule, and an A/B/n test are the same thing: **a name with variants, decided per visitor**. One primitive owns that decision — `flag()` — and because the decision happens **server-side during SSR**, the chosen variant is simply what gets rendered. No flicker, no client SDK, no JS shipped for the decision. A `csr = false` page A/B tests for free.

You read a flag by **calling it**. You branch with `pick`. You wire a vendor (OpenFeature) with one `decide()`. That's the whole surface.

## Declare — the second argument is the shape

```ts
import { flag } from 'ogygia/flag';

export const checkout = flag('new-checkout');                    // kill switch — off until decided on
export const promo    = flag('summer-promo', 10);                // number → sticky rollout to 10%
export const beta     = flag('beta', (c) => !!c.visitor?.beta);  // function → targeting
export const hero     = flag('hero', { control: 80, bold: 20 }); // record → weighted variants (ratios)
```

- **omitted** → a kill switch, off for everyone (the safe default).
- **a number** → the sticky percent of traffic that gets the feature.
- **a function** → per-request targeting: `true` = on, `false` = **definitively** off (a rollout can't re-include it), `undefined` = fall through. It runs per request, so read a cookie, an env var, a DB row — runtime toggles without a redeploy.
- **a record** → weighted variants. Weights are **ratios** (`{ control: 3, bold: 1 }` is 75/25); the first key is control.

## Read — by calling the flag

```ts
'/': page(Home, {
  load: (c) => ({
    dark: checkout(c),        // boolean
    variant: hero(c),         // 'control' | 'bold' — sticky per visitor
  }),
}),
```

The decision is **deterministic and sticky**: the same visitor gets the same answer on every request, from a hash of `(flag name, visitor id)` — no storage, no cookie writes, nothing to sync.

## Branch — `pick`, one verb, two forms

`pick` means "here's a map of variant → thing; give this visitor their entry."

**A page slot** (no `c` — the router supplies it per request):

```ts
import Hero from './Hero.svelte';
import HeroBold from './HeroBold.svelte';

'/': page(hero.pick({ control: Hero, bold: HeroBold }), { load }),
```

`$infer` never notices — the page's types come from the load, so the arms are free. The arms don't have to be different files: because a component's schedule rides its **import attribute**, two bindings of ONE file are two different pages — which answers "does full hydration actually beat islands?" as a real experiment:

```ts
import Lab from './Lab.svelte';                          // pure server HTML — zero JS
import LabLive from './Lab.svelte' with { wake: 'load' }; // the SAME file as one live island

export const csrMode = flag('csr-mode', { static: 50, hydrated: 50 });

'/lab': page(csrMode.pick({ static: Lab, hydrated: LabLive }), { load }),
```

**A value** (you have `c`, so pass it and get the answer):

```ts
const trialDays = pricing.pick(c, { control: 7, generous: 30 });    // 7 | 30, typed + total
const label     = hero.pick(c, { control: 'Buy now', bold: 'BUY →' });
const cfg        = hero.pick(c, {
  control: { columns: 2, video: false },
  bold:    { columns: 1, video: true },
});
```

TypeScript forces the map to cover every variant, and the return type is the union of its values. `pick` earns its keep at 3+ variants; for a boolean flag, just call it, and for two variants a ternary is fine.

## Who is the visitor?

Stickiness needs an identity. Declare it **once**, on the route table:

```ts
export const app = routes(table, {
  visitor: (c) => session_from(c.cookies),   // → { sub: 'u123', … } or undefined
});
```

That becomes [`c.visitor`](/docs/app/server-router) everywhere — flags stick on `c.visitor.sub`, and it's the same identity [mounts sign onward](/docs/app/federation) to other teams. Outside the router (or for anonymous traffic) the fallback is an `og-vid` cookie, then anonymous — and **anonymous visitors always see control**, so a split never flaps for someone you can't recognize.

For sticky rollouts on **logged-out traffic**, mint the cookie:

```ts
import { anonymousVisitor } from 'ogygia/router';

const anon = anonymousVisitor();                       // { cookie?, days? }
routes(table, { visitor: (c) => session(c) ?? anon(c) });
```

## Vendor values — `.value()`

A flag can carry a vendor-authored payload (config edited in the vendor's dashboard, not in code). Validate it with a schema — an invalid payload is **ignored**, never entered, the same trust-boundary law as a body schema:

```ts
import * as v from 'valibot';

export const banner = flag('promo-banner', 0, {
  value: v.object({ text: v.string(), tone: v.picklist(['info', 'warn']) }),
  fallback: { text: '', tone: 'info' },   // when the vendor is silent or the value is invalid
});

banner.value(c);   // typed payload — never undefined
```

## OpenFeature — `decide({ source })`

Native flags need no setup. To decide flags with a vendor instead, wire it **once**. ogygia depends on no vendor SDK — you hand `decide` your own OpenFeature client:

```ts title=hooks.server.ts
import { OpenFeature } from '@openfeature/server-sdk';
import { LaunchDarklyProvider } from '@launchdarkly/openfeature-node-server';
import { decide } from 'ogygia/flag';
import { openfeature } from 'ogygia/flag/openfeature';

OpenFeature.setProvider(new LaunchDarklyProvider(env.LD_KEY));
decide({ source: openfeature(OpenFeature.getClient()) });
```

The source is resolved **once per request** over exactly the flags your app declares, then every read stays sync — after that instant, a vendor-decided flag is indistinguishable from a native one (same calls, same `pick`, same [federation carry](#one-world-across-teams)). Decisions are validated against your declared variants; a vendor typo can't invent a bucket. If the vendor is slow or down, every flag degrades to its native rule — it never gates first byte.

No vendor SDK? Speak the OpenFeature remote protocol directly:

```ts
import { ofrep } from 'ogygia/flag/openfeature';
decide({ source: ofrep({ url: 'http://flagd:8016' }) });
```

Custom logic or a database? A source is just a function:

```ts
decide({ source: async (queries, c) => {
  const rows = await db.flags(c.visitor?.sub);
  return Object.fromEntries(rows.map((r) => [r.name, r.variant]));
}});
```

> On plain SvelteKit (no ogygia router), `import` your flags module in `hooks.server.ts` so every flag is registered before the first request primes the source. Router apps import all route modules at startup, so their registry is always complete.

## The precedence chain

Every read resolves in this order, first hit wins:

**`?og-exp` override → carried claims → the source → the native rule → control.**

- **override** — a QA force from the URL (below).
- **carried claims** — a decision an upstream shell already made (federation).
- **source** — the vendor, if `decide({ source })` set one.
- **native rule** — your targeting function / weights / rollout.
- **control** — the first variant (or `off`).

## QA override

Force any variant from the URL — it wins over everything, including stickiness:

```
/lab?og-exp=csr-mode:hydrated
/?og-exp=hero:bold,new-checkout:on
```

**Dev-only by default.** In production an open URL override would let any visitor force themselves into unfinished features and pollute your data — so out of the box, production ignores `?og-exp`. Opt QA in with something a visitor can't forge:

```ts title=hooks.server.ts
decide({ overrides: (c) => c.visitor?.roles?.includes('qa') ?? false });
```

## Mutual exclusion — the `layer` option

**The problem.** Two experiments touch the hero section: a redesign, and an autoplay video. If one visitor lands in BOTH treatments and conversions move, you cannot say which change did it — the two measurements contaminate each other. Experiments on the same surface must never share a visitor.

**The mechanism: lanes.** Flags sharing a `layer` string form a group:

```ts
const heroRedesign = flag('hero-redesign', { control: 50, bold: 50 }, { layer: 'hero-surface' });
const heroVideo    = flag('hero-video',    { control: 50, video: 50 }, { layer: 'hero-surface' });
```

For each visitor the layer makes ONE sticky decision, before any member's own logic runs: hash `(layer name, visitor id)` over the **sorted** member names. That picks the visitor's **owner** — picture all traffic split into N equal lanes, one lane per member. Reading a member flag then opens with a single question: *am I this visitor's owner?*

- **Not the owner** → answer control, immediately. The flag's targeting and weights never even run for that visitor.
- **The owner** → proceed exactly as if the layer didn't exist, inside your own lane.

Walkthrough: visitor `puru` hashes into `hero-video`'s lane. `heroRedesign(puru)` → `'control'`, always — its 50/50 never executes. `heroVideo(puru)` → its own 50/50. The same inputs hash the same way on every request and every replica (sorted names, so module import order can never flip a lane) — no storage, no cross-server coordination, nothing to sync.

**The arithmetic to remember.** A member only ever experiments inside its own lane, so its real exposure is its split **divided by the member count**: a 50% variant in a 2-member layer reaches 25% of all traffic; add a third member and every lane shrinks to a third.

**What outranks the layer.** The override, carried claims, and the vendor source all sit ABOVE the lane gate in the precedence chain — `?og-exp=hero-redesign:bold` shows QA the variant even for a visitor owned by the other member, an upstream shell's carried decision is honored as-is, and a vendor that answers for a flag bypasses the lane (the vendor owns exclusion then — don't run the same surface in both systems).

## Measurement — `decide({ exposure })`

ogygia ships **no metrics pipeline** — the decision is the primitive; counting is your analytics' job. The one seam is an exposure sink, fired once per request per flag on first read (under SSR, deciding *is* rendering the variant). Batching is built in:

```ts title=hooks.server.ts
decide({
  exposure: (events) => analytics.track(events),   // an array of { name, variant, sub, at }
  batch: { max: 25, ms: 2000 },                     // flushed at the cap, the timer, or request end
});
```

A throwing sink never breaks a request; the tail drains at each request's end (serverless-safe). For logs and `data-og-exp` stamps, `stamp(c)` gives `name:variant`:

```svelte
<section data-og-exp={data.stamp}>…</section>
```

## One world across teams

"Carried" in the precedence chain is the federation tier. Every flag a request **decides** self-registers its bucket, so when a shell [mounts other teams' apps](/docs/app/federation) the visitor's whole world travels in the signed claims — nobody hand-lists anything, and forgetting an entry can't silently fork a visitor between teams. A carried decision beats the local native rule, so one visitor never straddles two worlds.

A shell that only **routes** to teams (it never reads the flag itself) pre-decides it on the table, so the bucket still travels:

```ts
routes(table, { visitor: session, flags: [csrMode] });   // decide-but-don't-render
```

## The flag manifest

Every production build writes an inventory of the `flag()` call sites it compiled — AST-resolved (renamed and namespace imports count; an unrelated local function named `flag` doesn't) — to:

```
node_modules/.ogygia/flags-manifest.json   →  { flags: [{ name, kind, file, line }], names: [...] }
```

Flags rot. A code-first setup gets most of the industry's flag-debt tooling from the inventory alone — record the manifest in CI, diff it per release, and alarm on any name that outlives its retirement. The `ogygia_flags` MCP tool reads it live. Retiring a flag stays a human edit (delete the flag, keep the winning branch); the manifest tells you which ones are overdue.
